She had been in the news, there had been a lot going on in her life.
她频频登上新闻，她的生活里发生了很多事。

00:23:24.039 --> 00:23:30.680
This was sort of her coming back out of having, I think a bit of a hard time, and coming back and working, and doing something everyone was so curious.
这可以说是她经历了一段艰难时期后的一种回归，重新开始工作，做一些让所有人都很好奇的事情。

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:34.200
What's your guys' memory of how that part felt?
你们对当时那种感觉有什么记忆？

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:35.799
She was, by the way, great in the episode.
顺便说一句，她在这一集里演得很好。

00:23:35.799 --> 00:23:43.480
I was delighted by how beautiful and hilarious she was, and she was really good, and she was total, she was professional and great to work with.
她长得那么漂亮，又那么搞笑，这让我感到惊喜，而且她真的很棒，非常专业，合作起来也很愉快。

00:23:43.480 --> 00:23:48.480
But what was your memory of like, what, how strange that felt, the scrutiny left.
但你们对当时那种感觉有什么记忆？那种外界审视带来的怪异感。

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:53.640
Sarah, I have a very strong memory of something you said that made me laugh so hard.
萨拉，我对你说过的一句话记忆犹新，当时把我逗得大笑。

00:23:54.559 --> 00:24:08.680
Like, I think people, like Sarah and I were, like kind of getting known for being on TV, but like being a series regular on a beloved show is a whole different world than what the kind of like fame and scrutiny a Britney Spears was under.
就像，我觉得人们，就像萨拉和我当时，因为上电视而开始有点名气，但作为一部备受欢迎的剧集的常规演员，跟布兰妮·斯皮尔斯所承受的那种名气和审视，完全是两个不同的世界。

00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:15.799
Like it's like, I think we felt like a different species of a famous person had come to the set.
就像是，我觉得我们觉得有一种不同物种的名人来到了片场。

00:24:15.799 --> 00:24:18.779
That was not like a working actor kind of vibe.
那可不是那种职业演员的氛围。

00:24:18.779 --> 00:24:21.440
Like she was so iconically famous.
她有着标志性的巨大名气。

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:25.279
She was so at that point had been like kind of torn apart.
在那个时候，她几乎已经被媒体和公众折腾得遍体鳞伤了。

00:24:25.279 --> 00:24:26.960
Like my heart went out to her.
我的心当时都揪住了。

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:33.799
It was like really, and she was on this kind of like, trying to kind of repair things and come back.
这真的是……而且她正处于这样一种状态：试图修复一些事情并重新复出。

00:24:33.799 --> 00:24:37.599
And but I remember it was this feeling of like, what do we say to her?
而且但我记得当时有那种感觉，就是，我们要对她说什么？

00:24:37.599 --> 00:24:39.680
Like how do we communicate?
就像我们该怎么交流？

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:46.759
but I remember you came up to a couple of us and you said, well, I had like three things to talk about.
但我也记得你走到我们几个人面前说，嗯，我大概有三件事想聊。

00:24:46.759 --> 00:24:51.279
I talked about all of them and I'm out of stuff to talk about like you had like your things.
我把它们都聊完了，然后没东西可聊了，就像你列好了你要聊的事。

00:24:51.279 --> 00:24:52.720
You're like, you gave it a go.
就像是，你试过了。

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:55.599
And then you kind of ran out of things and you kind of came back to us.
然后你差不多把话题聊完了，就回到了我们这里。

00:24:55.599 --> 00:24:57.680
And it just really made me laugh.
这真的把我逗笑了。

00:24:57.680 --> 00:24:59.279
Do you remember that?
你还记得吗？

00:24:59.279 --> 00:25:23.529
I remember wanting to like, you know, have her feel comfortable because I imagined that, You know, that level of, I remember being kind of blown away by that level of scrutiny she's under, like the helicopter circling, just trying to get a picture of her, the fact that everybody had to wear wristbands onto the- I forgot about that, to get onto the stage.
我记得我很想，你知道的，让她感到放松，因为我想象到，你知道的，那种程度的……我记得当时对她所承受的那种审视程度感到有点震惊，比如直升机在盘旋，只是为了拍一张她的照片，事实上每个人都必须戴着腕带才能进——我忘了这个了，才能走到舞台上。

00:25:23.529 --> 00:25:25.970
To get onto the stage, because only time that ever happened.
才能走到舞台上，因为那是唯一一次发生这种事。

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:30.609
Only time that was like, I was like, oh, wow, this is pretty tight, tight, we run a tight ship here.
唯一一次有这种感觉，当时我觉得，哦，哇，这真是相当严格，我们这里管理得很严格。

00:25:30.609 --> 00:25:32.009
We don't have wristbands at Scrum.
我们在《周六夜现场》不戴腕带。

00:25:32.009 --> 00:25:33.529
But then I realized, oh, that's what we did this week.
但后来我意识到，哦，这就是我们这周做的事。

00:25:33.529 --> 00:25:37.210
And like, it is not the norm, and that it's because obviously she runs into that.
而且，这并不是常态，这是因为她显然会遇到这种情况。

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:51.089
And like, I can't imagine what it would be like to live under that level of scrutiny all of the time and to not just be a person that you meet, but to be like, you know, somebody who only needs to go by a first name and everybody knows who you're talking about.
而且，我无法想象一直生活在那样的审视之下会是什么感觉，而且不仅仅是你遇到普通人，而是那种，你知道的，只需要一个名字，所有人就知道你在说谁的人。

00:25:51.089 --> 00:25:54.890
And yeah, I remember that.
是的，我记得。

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:57.569
I remember we talked about, I think, pets.
我记得我们聊过，我想，是宠物。

00:25:57.569 --> 00:26:06.621
And then I remember my other memory of her was like, of her was like, you know, she was really, obviously doesn't have like a big background in comedy and she did so great.
然后我还记得我对她的另一个记忆是，关于她的是，你知道的，她真的，显然在喜剧方面没有什么大背景，但她做得太棒了。

00:26:06.621 --> 00:26:15.040
Like that scene when she's, when you know, she's like, I'll let her know when you're asking to see Stella and she's like, and you're like, what, telepathically?
就像那场戏，当她，你知道的，她说“我会让她知道你想见斯特拉”，然后她说，而你却说：“什么，用心灵感应吗？”

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:16.356
Because she doesn't move.
因为她一动不动。

00:26:16.356 --> 00:26:19.461
And then her laugh after and she's like, that's funny, dad.
然后她笑了一下说：“这挺好笑的，爸爸。”

00:26:19.461 --> 00:26:21.040
That's funny, smart and great.
又好笑、又聪明又棒。

00:26:21.039 --> 00:26:22.391
That's funny, smart and great.
又好笑、又聪明又棒。

00:26:22.391 --> 00:26:23.428
Funny, smart and great.
好笑、聪明又棒。

00:26:23.428 --> 00:26:24.960
That was my favorite line of hers.
那是我最喜欢她的台词。

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:25.480
Me too.
我也是。

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:26.596
And so genuine.
而且那么真诚。

00:26:26.596 --> 00:26:33.440
And like, I remember thinking like, wow, that's like, cool that she can come in and do that.
而且，我记得当时想，哇，她能来并做到这一点，真是太酷了。

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:34.599
Do you know what I remember, correct?
你知道我记得什么吗，克雷格？

00:26:34.599 --> 00:26:44.559
You know what I thought when I was watching the show, Craig, was I thought you guys did her a really big service, which was you didn't spotlight her as the center of the show.
你知道我在看节目时是怎么想的吗，克雷格，我觉得你们帮了她一个很大的忙，那就是你们没有把她作为节目的中心聚光灯。

00:26:44.559 --> 00:26:47.759
She was like a side comedic character in the show.
她在剧里就像是一个喜剧配角。

00:26:47.759 --> 00:26:52.920
Incredibly well used, incredibly funny, but she's not part of the main romance of the show.
用得极其绝妙，极其搞笑，但她并不是剧里主要浪漫线的一部分。

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:54.599
That was Sarah and I.
那是莎拉和我。

00:26:54.599 --> 00:26:55.920
Yeah.
对。

00:26:55.920 --> 00:27:00.500
And she also was like, she wasn't the object of desire.
而且她也不是那种被渴望的对象。

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:01.900
she was the one who was yearning.
她反而是那个充满渴望的人。

00:27:01.900 --> 00:27:07.779
Yeah, and I think that was so smart to take her because she'd been so objectified her whole life.
对，我觉得这样安排太聪明了，因为她一辈子都被人对象化了。

00:27:07.779 --> 00:27:19.740
Yeah, since she was a teenager to turn her into this incredibly sweet, earnest character that was, you just didn't slather her with her persona.
对，从她还是个十几岁的女孩开始，把她变成这样一个极其甜美、认真的角色，你根本不会把她过去的人设生搬硬套到她身上。

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:25.500
Yeah, and I think it allowed her to give like this really sharp, really funny character turn.
对，我觉得这让她能够呈现出一种非常犀利、非常有趣的性格转变。

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:26.339
Yeah, I agree.
对，我同意。

00:27:26.339 --> 00:27:27.539
That's a good point.
这话说得好。

00:27:27.539 --> 00:27:28.380
She leaned into it.
她完全融入进去了。

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.019
So her, she was like, she knew that assignment.
所以她，她就像是，她懂那个任务。

00:27:31.019 --> 00:27:34.940
She was like, I'm longing for this guy who's looking right past me.
她就像是，我渴望这个对我视若无睹的家伙。

00:27:34.940 --> 00:27:37.019
And I'm gonna act that part and be in Aussie.
而我要演好这个角色，扮演奥茜。

